## 1. Introduction
Climate change, an undeniable concern that poses a major risk to humanity and ecological systems alike, stems from greenhouse their highly crystalline nature, tunable structures, and record porosity.[11–13] Unfortunately, many MOFs also suffer from certain issues, such as low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity and selectivity, particularly in the presence of humidity,[14,15] and short lifetime.
Thus, to enhance their performance, numerous efforts have been made to develop post-synthetic modification (PSM) strategies that can be used to decorate the inside of MOFs with basic groups, like amines. Such studies are predominately focused on impregnation approaches, where low molecular weight molecules or polymers are allowed to diffuse into the MOF pores.[16–18] In such studies, the amines are often adsorbed on the internal framework wall and/or onto open metal coordination sites.[18–20] However, when such interactions are weak, they can lead to amine leaching, particularly in humid environments, limiting performance with extensive cycling.[21] For instance, Long et al. reported the displacement of lightweight amines from the metal surfaces in an amine-functionalized MOF, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) (dobpdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dihydroxy biphenyl dicarboxylic acid), in humid flue gas streams during temperature swing processes used for material regeneration.[21] This was overcome by introducing tetraamines that were crosslinked between two $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ centers in the MOF.[22,23] Another approach is to use alkyl amine-based ligands or alkyl amine-based metal building units for the synthesis of a desired MOF. For instance, Yaghi et al., synthesized IRMOF-74-III- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ using an alkyl amine-based ligand, $2^{\prime}$ -(aminomethyl)- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime\prime}}$ -dihydroxy-[1,1’:4’,1’’-terphenyl]- $\cdot4,4^{\prime\prime}$ - dicarboxylic acid, to boost the material’s $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ performance in the presence of humidity.[24,25] The material offered no loss in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ performance in $65\%$ RH compared to the non-amine functionalized IRMOF-74-III. In another report, Moon et al., used alkyl amine-based metal building units; in this case, a Ni (II) macrocyle (referred to as $[\mathrm{NiL_{ethylamine}}]^{2+})$ that contained two pendent ethylamines was combined with a biphenyl dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) ligand to synthesize a MOF (referred as $\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{NH}2}$ ) that had amine functionality tethered to the metal building unit.[26] The resulting material, $\mathrm{MOF}_{\mathrm{NH}2}$ , was reported to be stable in a dry simulated flue gas stream for 12 cycles with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of $4.8\mathrm{wt.}\%$ . Albeit such approaches lead to nice results, they cannot often be universally applied to different MOFs.
Another strategy used to enhance MOF performance in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture entails post-synthetically grafting amines onto the backbone of MOF ligands. Unfortunately, to date there are few articles reporting amine grafting to MOF ligands,[27–30] and, of the existing studies, few have led to significant enhancements in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake in the pressure regime of interest for post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. For example, while ED (ethylenediamine) was grafted onto the amine ligand in UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ MOF,[28] no significant enhancement in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity in the low-pressure regime was observed. In another report, PEI (polyethylene imine) was reportedly grafted onto an amine ligand in the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF. While there was enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at low pressures,[29] there was no experimental evidence of successful grafting, and minimal cycling data was shown. Albeit not described here, post-synthetic grafting has also been carried out in other porous materials, such as covalent organic frameworks (COFs)[31] and porous organic polymers (POPs).[32] Notably, we previously reported a new strategy to graft amines to the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - BDC (amino-terephthalic acid) ligand inside of a MOF, $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (also known as IRMOF-3), using bromoacetyl bromide as a bridging molecule.[30] Albeit the grafting could be quantified, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity in the low-pressure regime was minimally enhanced relative to the parent MOF (0.13 to 0.16 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{at}0.15$ bar and $313\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively). Moreover, the material was highly moisture sensitive making its application in humid gas separations impossible. Thus, in the present work, we report the same PSM strategy[30] carried out in a more robust MOF. The MOF, known as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC, (alternatively known as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-101-Cr) was modified with four different amines of varying length and size, including ethylenediamine (ED), diethylenetriamine (DETA), tris(2-aminoethyl)amine (TAEA) and Polyethylene imine (PEI, $800\ensuremath{\mathrm{MW}}$ ). Next, the modified materials were assessed for post-combustion carbon capture. Notably, there is a positive correlation between the density of primary amines and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, selectivity, and binding energy. Interestingly, the amine composites also offered an improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation in a simulated flue gas mixture containing $15\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $85\%$ $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ ( $\approx80\%$ RH). Further, the materials offer impressive performance over 200 continuous adsorption/desorption cycles. Last, after soaking the two best-performing materials (TAEA and PEI grafted MOFs) in water, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity is found to remain the same with no signs of amine leaching. On the contrary, when the same MOF is impregnated with low molecular weight amines (TAEA) using traditional approaches, significant leaching is observed.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterizations of Amine Composites
$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC was synthesized via a reported protocol[33] (See the Experimental Section). The material is constructed from 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC})$ and $\operatorname{Cr}_{3}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{O})$ core forming a MOF having the following chemical formula: $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O(X)(H_{2}O)}_{2}(\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{\cdotBDC^{2-}})_{3}$ $\mathrm{(X=OH^{-}}$ or ${\mathrm{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ ). The assynthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC MOF is crystalline and phase pure (Figure S1a, Supporting Information); the broad diffraction peaks come from the nano-size of the crystals, which have an average size of $34.0\pm6.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S1c, Supporting Information). The MOF was activated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under dynamic vacuum to access the surface area and pore volume. Nitrogen adsorption measurements, carried out at $77~\mathrm{K},$ reveal a BET surface area $(\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}})$ of ${\approx}2400{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $1.6\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S1b, Supporting Information). The MOF exhibits a 3D porous structure with two different pore windows that are ${\approx}1.2$ nm and $1.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter[33] and cages that are ${\approx}2.7~\mathrm{nm}$ and $3.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter (Figure S1b, Supporting Information, inset); these structural features can facilitate the diffusion of reagents in and out of the material.
For PSM-1, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC was mixed with 1, 2, or 3 equivalents of bromoacetyl bromide (BrAcBr) per ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{BDC}}^{2-}$ ligand in THF, which was stirred for $60\mathrm{min}$ in an ice bath and then $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature (RT). The concentration of BrAcBr was varied to maximize the appendage of BrAcBr onto the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC2− ligands. The obtained product, denoted as BrAc-NH-Cr-BDC, was still crystalline and pure as indicated by the PXRD pattern (Figure S2a, Supporting Information). The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption at 77 K showed a gradual drop in the $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ from ${\approx}2400{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ observed for the bare $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC to 1785, 1620 and $1530~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , after treating with 1 equiv., 2 equiv. and 3 equiv. of BrAcBr, respectively (Figure S2b, Supporting Information); this drop likely indicates the incorporation of organics in the pore. The proof of functionalization was further confirmed using Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR). A band representative of a carbonyl $(-\mathrm{C=O})$ stretching vibration of the amide appears at $1705\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after PSM-1 (Figure 1a). In addition to this, the two stretching vibrations associated with primary amines $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ at 3388 and $3500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ decreased in intensity after PSM-1, and a new band, which is representative of the stretching vibration of secondary amines $\scriptstyle(-\mathrm{NH-})$ , appears at $3300\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure 1a); this confirms the successful transformation of the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{BDC}}^{2-}$ ligand into $\mathrm{BrAc-NH\cdotBDC^{2-}}$ . Unfortunately, despite being treated with an excess of BrAcBr (3 equiv.), the stretching vibration of the primary amine on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC remains, implying that there is less than $100\%$ ligand conversion. So, further insight into the nature of the functionalization was provided by the $\mathrm{Br}3\mathrm{d}$ region of XPS data.[34] Notably, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC treated with 3 equiv. of BrAcBr has intense peaks at binding energies (BEs) of 70.37 and 71.42 eV $(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively), which are assigned to C─Br interactions (Figure 1c, blue) and indicative of successful grafting. Interestingly, additional peaks were also observed at lower BEs of 68.16 and $69.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively), which were assigned to inorganic Br species $(\mathrm{NH_{3}^{\mathrm{+}}B r^{\mathrm{-}}}$ and $\operatorname{Cr-Br})$ ) (Figure 1c, red). However, because the BEs of these proposed inorganic Br species are similar,[35,36] it is difficult to distinguish them via XPS analysis. While the complexity associated with the different possible inorganic species makes fully understanding the chemistry and quantifying the speciation after PSM-1 challenging, the XPS data could be fit to quantify the amount of organic and inorganic Br; the results indicate that there is a contribution from $\approx54\%$ organic species (C─Br) and a $46\%$ inorganic
Br species $(\mathrm{Cr-Br}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{3}^{\mathrm{+}}B r^{-}}$ ) (See Table S1, Supporting Information).
It is noted that, for every ligand modification with BrAcBr in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC, one $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ is released, which then has an opportunity to protonate unreacted $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC ligands or the anion could bind to the $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ cluster via exchange with the $\mathrm{OH^{-}/N O_{3}^{-}}$ . Knowing this, EXAFS (Extended X-ray Absorption Fine Structure) data was then collected on BrAc-NH-Cr-BDC to extract structural information on the atoms in close proximity to Br (Figure 1d). To further explore the complexes formed during the functionalization of the MOF with BrAcBr, Kohn-Sham density functional methods (DFT) were employed. For this, the MOF system was reduced to cluster models representative of the pristine MOF with the $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ counter ion exchanged with $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information) and the MOF after PSM-1 (Figure 1e). The methods used in the complementary computational study are well described in the SI. In Figure 1e, the framework after PSM-1 is modeled by a cluster containing $-\mathrm{NH-AcBr}$ and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ functionalization on two benzoates as well as $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ that are released during the modification.[37] It was found in the simulations that the $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ ion binds in close proximity to the ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ site, which is also adjacent to the unreacted, but protonated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC ligand; this indicates that the $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ could have a dual electrostatic interaction with both ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{3}}^{+}$ (Figure 1e). The optimized atom distances of the simulated cluster model (Figure 1e) were then compared to the radial distances obtained from the EXAFS spectrum. The peaks found at 1.93 Å, $2.54\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , and $3.1\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in the EXAFS data are in close correlation with the $\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-Br}}(1.967{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}})$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ -Br $(2.605\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and $\mathrm{NH_{3}}^{+}$ -Br $(3.295\mathring\mathrm{A})$ distances (Figure 1d,e) obtained from DFT. The DFT calculation also suggests that the interaction between $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{3}}^{+}$ on the adjacent ligand, weakens the $\mathrm{Cr-Br}$ interaction. This can be observed by a slight elongation of the $\mathrm{Cr-Br}$ bond in the PSM-1 cluster when compared to the $\mathrm{Cr-Br}$ distance of $2.48\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ found in the cluster model of the pristine MOF (Figure S4, Supporting Information).
With $46\%$ of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC ligands functionalized with BrAcBr, PSM-2 was subsequently carried out. This process consisted of a reaction between BrAc-NH- $\mathrm{Cr}$ -BDC and 3 equivalents of various alkylamines (Scheme 1). The reaction employed ED, DETA, TAEA, or PEI in THF for $60\mathrm{min}$ at $1{-}4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then $30\mathrm{min}$ at RT. The successful conversion of BrAc-NH- $\mathrm{Cr}$ -BDC to AmineAc-NH-Cr-BDC was then probed by $\mathrm{Br}3\mathrm{d}$ region of XPS data. For this, one of the amine-grafted composites, TAEA-Ac-NH- $\mathrm{Cr}$ -BDC was used, which showed only a very minor contribution of ${\approx}2\%$ of ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-Br}}$ and $98\%$ of inorganic Br $(\mathrm{Cr}{-}\mathrm{Br}/\mathrm{NH_{3}}{+}\mathrm{Br^{-}})$ (Figure 1f; Table S1, Supporting Information). This indicates that there is ${\approx}98\%$ conversion of the BrAc-functionalized ligands to the amine functionalized ones, meaning a total of $\approx45\%$ of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC ligands are functionalized with amines. All four resulting products obtained after PSM-2 were found to be crystalline and phase pure as indicated by PXRD (Figure S5a, Supporting Information). Next, the materials were subsequently activated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vacuum to access the $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and pore volume after amine functionalization. The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption at $77~\mathrm{K}$ revealed $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ ranging from ${\approx}320$ to $1570\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after amine grafting, implying that the materials are still highly porous (Figure S6 and Table S2, Supporting Information). It is noted that the drop in surface area correlates with the quantity and size of the added amine; for instance, the $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ drop was higher for the bulkiest amine, PEI, while it was lower for TAEA ( $[0.46\mathrm{nm}$ radii) and the linear amines, DETA ( $0.8\mathrm{nm}$ length) and ED ( $0.54\mathrm{nm}$ length). TGA also revealed higher organic content for the composite produced from PEI $({\approx}56~\mathrm{wt}\%)$ grafting than for TAEA, DETA, and ED (Figure S7 and Table S2, Supporting Information). Notably, the Amine-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC samples had $6{-}7~\mathrm{wt}\%$ quantities of $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ still in the pores as indicated by IC; so, it was hypothesized that the samples still contained a large quantity of protonated amines (Table S3, Supporting Information).[30] Owed to our desire to employ the materials in post-combustion carbon capture, the amine appended samples were washed with $N,N\mathrm{.}$ Diisopropylethylamine (DIPEA) in MeOH for $30\mathrm{min}$ . This process was meant to deprotonate residual amine-based salts in the structure as such species have a lower affinity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ relative to their deprotonated counterparts.[30] The washed products, denoted as Amine-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, were found to be crystalline and phase pure (Figure S5b, Supporting Information). Importantly, a significant decrease in the $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ content to less than $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ was observed for all composites via IC after the DIPEA wash (Table S3, Supporting Information). The elemental analysis also showed a slight increase in N and C content due to the removal of $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ (Table S3, Supporting Information). This is further supported by TGA data, which reveals a decrease in the residual weight $\%$ for all composites, for example, from 36.9 to $34.6\mathrm{wt\%}$ , after burning off organics in TAEA-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC after washing. This decrease in residual mass implies that the net Br content is reduced after the wash (Figure S7b and Table S2, Supporting Information). The decrease in the Br content is further supported by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption data collected at $77~\mathrm{K}$ ; the $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ slightly increased for DETA and TAEA functionalized MOF by ${\approx}100~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S6 and Table S2, Supporting Information) after the DIPEA washing. However, no significant change in surface area was observed for the ED functionalized MOF.
## 2.2. $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Adsorption Studies
After PSM-2, used to graft amines inside the MOF pores, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance of each material was studied. For this, the amine-functionalized MOFs, both before and after the DIPEA wash, were activated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under dynamic vacuum. Next, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was measured at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ , a temperature of interest for post-combustion carbon capture. Among the small-chain polyamines, including ED, DETA, and TAEA, the best-performing material is TAEA-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC, which has a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of 0.8 mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar (Figure S8a, Supporting Information) and is further increased to 1 mmol $\mathsf{C O}_{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ with the DIPEA wash (Figure 2a; Figure S8b, Supporting Information). The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake was enhanced for all amine composites upon $\mathrm{Br^{-}}$ removal by the DIPEA wash (Figure 2a; Figure S8a, Supporting Information). This stems from the fact that the base wash transforms the amine salts (like $\mathrm{NH}_{3}{}^{+}\mathrm{Br}^{-})$ )
into the deprotonated form $\left(\mathrm{R-NH}_{2}\right)$ , which has a higher affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . For comparison, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar is 0.48, 0.7, and $1.0\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ for ED-Ac, DETA-Ac- and TAEAAc-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, respectively. Further, the better performance of TAEA-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed is related to the higher density of primary amines when compared to ED and DETA. Given this, it was hypothesized that other polyamines having more primary amines would further boost capacity. To test this, PEI having a molecular weight of $800\ \mathrm{g\mol^{-1}}$ was also grafted onto the MOF ligand forming PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC. Albeit that PXRD indicates that the PEI grafted composite is crystalline, the low angle peaks are suppressed likely due to pore filling, which is a common behavior observed for PEI incorporated into the same porous frameworks[16] (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Despite this, as hypothesized, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake was enhanced to 1.18 and $1.55\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.15 bar and $313\mathrm{~K~}$ for the PEI-Ac-NH-CrBDC and PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, respectively; this is nearly $35\%$ higher than the TAEA-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed composite (Figure 2a; Figure S8a, Supporting Information). We note that the uptake capacity of PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC is much higher than we have previously observed when doing similar amine grafting in $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (alternatively known as IRMOF-3).[30] This indicates that the MOF template plays a strong role in amine loading and hence the resulting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity. Also, despite the enhancement observed for TAEA-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, the amine-grafting was only successful on ${\approx}45\%$ of the ligands, and hence, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of the material is still lower than several other materials reported in the literature.[16,17,20,21,23] It is hypothesized that modifying $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC, for instance, to incorporate mesoporous defects or employing other porous templates having larger pore sizes or volumes will help further boost the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities into a more competitive regime.
Next, the kinetic performance of the amine-Ac-NH-Cr-BDCwashed samples was assessed in a simulated post-combustion flue gas stream containing $15\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $85\%\mathrm{N}_{2}$ under dry and humid conditions $80\%$ relative humidity, RH). For this, breakthrough (BT) studies were conducted at $313\mathrm{K}$ by flowing the simulated flue gas mixture through a column of length $10.5\mathrm{cm}$ containing ${\approx}0.2\ \mathrm{g}$ of the activated MOF composite and ${\approx}0.2\ \mathrm{g}$ of glass beads. For the humid tests, the bed was pre-saturated with $80\%$ RH for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ using a He flow rate of $2\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , prior to passing a humid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ (15/85) gas mixture through the adsorbent column. For the parent MOF, the breakthrough separation time was $10\mathrm{ming^{-1}}$ in a dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ mixture and $3\mathrm{ming^{-1}}$ in the humid stream (Figure 3a). This decline is likely due to competitive adsorption of water molecules over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at the available adsorption sites. On the contrary, ED-Ac-, DETA-Ac-, TAEA-Ac- and PEI-AcNH-Cr-BDC-washed had much-improved breakthrough separation times of 15, 25, 61, and $70\mathrm{min}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, in dry conditions (Figure 3). In humid conditions there is an even greater enhancement as the breakthrough times increase to 25, 52, 74, and $90\mathrm{min}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for ED-Ac- to DETA-Ac- to TAEA-Ac- to PEI-AcNH-Cr-BDC-washed, respectively (Figure 3a–e and Table 1). It is noted that these values are 8, 17, 25, and 30 times larger than the original parent MOF, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC, that was also tested in the wet flue gas stream (Figure 3). The effective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity was also calculated from breakthrough data (Figure S14, Table S8, see Supporting Information for details). The best-performing materials, TAEA-Ac- and PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, have capacities of ${\approx}1.05$ and $1.23~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, in humid conditions (Table S8, Supporting Information). Whereas in dry conditions, the capacity is found to be slightly lower, 0.87 and $0.97\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for TAEA-Ac- and PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, respectively. On the contrary, the bare MOF has a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of $\mathrm{0.24mmol{g}^{-1}}$ in the dry mixture, and the value decreased to $0.097\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in the humid gas mixture. The reason for the enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of the amine composites in humid streams is due to the formation of bicarbonates/carbamic acid species, which involves an amine to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ratio of 1:1; however, in dry conditions, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption occurs via carbamate formation, which involves an amine to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ratio of 2:1 (Figure S15, Supporting Information).[39] Thus, in humid streams, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity is boosted. Last, the trend observed for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ kinetic separation performance (ED-Ac$<$ DETA-Ac- $\angle$ TAEA-Ac- $<$ PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed) expectedly correlates well with the trends observed for various thermodynamic parameters, including the low-pressure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity.
Next, the cyclability of the best performing materials were tested in a simulated flue gas stream. For this, TAEA-Ac- and PEIAc-NH-Cr-BDC-washed were subjected to 200 rapid TSA cycles. Adsorption was carried out in ${\approx}15\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $85\%\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ( $80\%$ RH) at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while desorption was done at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in highly humid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (with 7 min adsorption and desorption cycles). Given that both water and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorb at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the humid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake is presented in terms of overall wt% (Figure S24, Supporting Information). Notably, after the first few cycles (≈up to 25), the adsorption has a nearly $2-3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ loss for both samples (Figures S16 and $17\mathrm{a}$ , Supporting Information); this could stem from two scenarios. First, strongly adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and/or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ may not be released at the desorption temperature of $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Second, there could also be amine degradation, for instance, via the irreversible formation of urea species. Notably, previous reports show that humidity and lower desorption temperature (less than $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) can inhibit such amine degradation, and instead promote the formation of carbamate/bicarbonate species.[39,40] While the FTIR measurement provides no conclusive indication of amine degradation (urea or amide formation), changes in the intensities of IR band is observed in the range $1560{-}1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after cycling that are associated with NH deformations, which occur when amines react with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure S18, Supporting Information).[41] Albeit that the data also shows a slight shift in the $-\mathrm{C}=0$ stretching vibration of the amine-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC after cycling, from $1684~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ to $1669-$ $1674\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Figure S18, Supporting Information), we are unable to state with any certainty whether this results from additional Hbonding interactions between adsorbed water and $-\mathrm{C}=0$ grafted to the ligand or the formation of linear urea/carbamic acid.[40,42]
To better highlight the IR bands associated with irreversibly adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ species, the ATR-FTIR spectra of the fresh TAEAAc- functionalized MOF was subtracted from the same sample after 200 humid TSA cycles, (Figure S19b, Supporting Information). Notably, there is an intense IR band $\approx1667~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ after humid cycles that could be associated wtih the $-\mathrm{C}=0$ stretch of H-bonded carbamic acid/ carbamate $(1680-1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ),[42] O─H bend of adsorbed water molecules $(1635-1650~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}.$ ),[43] or the $-\mathrm{C}=0$ stretch of irreversible linear urea $(1640{-}1680~\mathrm{cm^{-1}},$ ).[40] Hence, it is inconclusive if amine degradation to linear urea occurs during humid cycling carried out with a $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ desorption temperature. However, there is the appearance of a new intense peak at ${\approx}1364~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ after the humid cycles indicative of symmetric $\mathtt{C-O}$ stretching of bicarbonate species $\mathrm{(HCO_{3}^{-})}$ (Figure S19b, Supporting Information).[44] Notably, as expected, this peak is not observed after dry cycles (Figure S24, red, Supporting Information); instead, there is a symmetric $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ stretch associated with carbamate species found at ${\approx}1346~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (Figure S19b,
Supporting Information).[45] Last, the broad IR bands observed in the range of $1500{-}1635~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is associated with chemisorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ species including, the asymmetric $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ stretch of carbamate $(1545-1570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-N}}$ stretch of carbamates (1515 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ), asymmetric $\mathrm{NH_{3}}^{+}$ stretches and NH deformation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $1560{-}1635\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}},$ ) (Figure S19b, Supporting Information). [42] Given this, we can confirm that the sharp capacity loss up to 25 cycle (as indicated by an increasing baseline), is at least partly attributed to chemisorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ species including bicarbonates and ammonium carbamates/carbamic acid species that are not released during the desorption step at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in humid cycles.
After cycle 25, the base line in the TGA plot stabilizes for both materials (Figure S16 and S17b, Supporting Information), indicating that there is minimal amine leaching (as would be indicated by a dropping baseline) or the irreversed formation of carbamates or bicarbonates (as would be indicated by a rising baseline). Despite this, there is still a slight decrease in the overall quantity of adsorbed species, by $\approx1.5$ and $\approx0.6$ wt. $\%$ for TAEAAc- and PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, respectively, between cycles 25 and 200. We hypothesize that this could stem from three phenomena. First, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration could gradually decrease over time due to dilution by the counter gas stream (pure $\Nu_{2}$ ) or absorption in the water bubbler. Second, there could be a slow degradation of the MOF during the rapid TSA cycles that blocks amine access. Third, the amines could gradually transform into other species, like imines, owed to oxygen impurities present during the TGA experiment. $[46,47]$ Notably, the PXRD patterns of the samples after cycling do not indicate MOF degradation (Figure S20, Supporting Information). In an effort to eliminate the possibility of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dilution, the cycling experiment of TAEA-Ac-NH$\mathrm{Cr}$ -BDC-washed was stopped and restarted after cycle 200 and then continued to cycle 270. It is noted that the material’s adsorption capacity indeed recovers to some degree, albeit incomplete recovery (Figure S21, Supporting Information). Unfortunately, from the FTIR data, it is not possible to determine if the amines transform into imine species, since the $\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}$ (imine) vibrations at $1670\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ lie in the same range as that of the $-\mathrm{C}=0$ stretch/O─H bend present in our system. [47] Thus, at this stage, we believe that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dilution and amine transformation could both play a role in the dropping performance after cycle 25. However, more extensive cycling studies under varying desorption conditions are required in the future for us to better understand the mechanism behind the performance decline.
Last, to prove that the amines are truly grafted to the MOF ligand and they do not leach out, the best performing materials, including TAEA-Ac- and PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed were soaked in water for $^{1\mathrm{{h}}}$ at RT, and subsequently vacuum dried. As expected, the framework remained intact (Figure S22, Supporting Information), and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms measured at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ show no loss of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity when compared to the as-synthesized amine grafted material (Figure 4). For comparison, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC MOF was also impregnated with TAEA and PEI using traditional approaches that employ cyclohexane as a solvent;[17] afterward, the impregnated samples were subjected to the same soaking treatment. In this case, there is a significant decline in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ performance for both materials. For instance, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of TAEA-impregnated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{Cr}$ -BDC was $2.5\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.15 bar and $313\ \mathrm{K};$ however, after soaking in water for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity drops to just $0.4\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is an ${\approx}85\%$ decline (Figure S23a, Supporting Information). For PEI, due to the high viscosity and insolubility of PEI in cyclohexane, the material became more like a slurry (Figure S23b, Supporting Information, inset image). Thus, the initial $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms could not be measured due to amine leaching during the measurements. However, the obtained slurry was subjected to the same treatment in water for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar was found to be $1.1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is already $30\%$ lower than that of PEI grafted composite, PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed, 1.55 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S23b, Supporting Information). Thus, this study highlights that impregnated amines can readily leach from MOF pores, while covalently grafting the amines can inhibit leaching, and hence enhance the lifetime of the material.
## 3. Conclusion
The work presented here demonstrates a two-step strategy used to covalently graft amines inside the pores of a robust MOF known as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC, for post-combustion carbon capture applications. In this work, bromoacetyl bromide (BrAcBr) was used as a bridge between the amine-decorated MOF ligand and several alkyl amines including ethylenediamine (ED), diethylenetriamine (DETA), tris(2-aminoethyl) amine (TAEA), and lowmolecular-weight polyethyleneimine (PEI). Various performance metrics, including $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity, and isosteric heats of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption $(Q_{s t})$ , were obtained for the parent MOF and each amine-appended counterpart. Notably, the aminedecorated materials offered a significant improvement in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance relative to the parent MOF. Moreover, as the number of primary amines and overall amine density was increased, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ performance was readily enhanced. The best performer in all tests was the sample denoted as PEI-Ac-NH-CrBDC-washed, which had the highest density of primary amines. The material offered $1.55\mathrm{\mmol{g}}^{-1}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at 313 K and 0.15 bar, an IAST $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity of 437.0, and a $Q_{\mathrm{st}}{\mathrm{of}}{\mathrm{-}}92\mathrm{kJ}{\mathrm{mol}}^{\mathrm{-}1}$ . Also, the breakthrough time was improved by a factor of 30 relative to the parent MOF when tested under humid conditions and a high cyclable capacity of $6.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was achieved even after 200 TSA cycles. Finally, by covalently grafting amines to the ligand, there was no change in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for materials before and after soaking in water; however, when compared to the same material simply impregnated with amines using traditional approaches, there was a significant loss in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, up to ${\approx}85\%$ , after soaking in water. Thus, it is hoped that amine grafting strategies like this one, can offer future benefits in terms of adsorbent lifetime, when compared to adsorbents where amines are simply physiosorbed in the pores of the porous support. Last, it is noted that less than $50\%$ of the ligands in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC were grafted with amines. This likely indicates that the MOF structure may inhibit full conversion. Thus, it is hypothesized that modifying $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC, for instance, to incorporate mesoporous defects, or employing other porous templates having larger pore sizes or volumes may help further boost the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities into a more competitive regime.
## 4. Experimental Section
All chemicals were obtained from commercial sources and used as received without further purification. Chromium nitrate nonahydrate, $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}.9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (Acros, $98\%$ , 2-aminoterephthalic acid, ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -BDC (ABCR, $98\%$ , bromoacetyl bromide, BrAcBr (ABCR, $98\%$ ), tetrahydrofuran, THF (Roth, $99.5\%\AA$ , N,N-dimethylformamide, DMF (Roth, $99.5\%\AA$ ), Methanol, MeOH (Roth, ${>}99\%$ ), Cyclohexane, anhydrous (Sigma-Aldrich, $99.5\ \%)$ Ethylenediamine, ED (TCI, $98\%$ ) Diethylenetriamine, DETA (TCI, $98\%$ ), Tris(2-aminoethyl)amine, TAEA (ABCR, $98\%$ ), Polyethyleneimine, PEI (800 MW) (Sigma–Aldrich, $99\%$ ), and $N,N$ -Diisopropylethylamine, DIPEA (ABCR, $98\%$ ).
Synthesis of $N H_{2}$ -Cr-BDC: A $760~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon autoclave was charged with $6.4~\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ . ${\mathfrak{s H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{:}$ $2.898\ \mathrm{g}$ of 2-amino-terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - ${\mathsf{B D C}}^{2-})$ ), $\overline{{1.28~\mathrm{g}}}$ of sodium hydroxide, and $720~\mathsf{m L}$ DI water. The mixture was first stirred for 15 min and sonicated for 15 min prior to transferring to an oven. The oven was ramped to ${150~^{\circ}C}$ in $\textsf{l h}$ , held at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ and cooled to $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in $4h$ . Once the reaction was finished, the bright green product was separated into four $50~\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tubes and washed with $40~\mathsf{m L}$ water once, $40~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF three times, and $40~\mathrm{mL}$ EtOH three times and then refluxed with $200~\mathrm{mL}$ EtOH at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\ h$ two times. The resulting product was vacuum dried at RT overnight and further vacuum dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ . The sample was activated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\ h$ under dynamic vacuum to access the surface area and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption properties.
Synthesis of Bromoacetyl Functionalized MOF (PSM-1): For the first step of PSM, $440~\mathsf{m g}$ of as-synthesized ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -MIL101(Cr) was dispersed in $20~\mathsf{m L}$ THF. The temperature was adjusted to $1{-}4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with an ice bath. Then, different equivalents of bromoacetyl bromide, BrAcBr (1 equiv., 140 $\upmu\upnu$ equiv., $280~\upmu\upiota$ and 3 equiv., $420~\upmu\up L)$ with respect to the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -BDC ligand were added to the cold solution and allowed to stir for $\textsf{l h}$ in the ice bath and then at RT for an additional $30~\mathrm{min}$ . After the reaction, the product was centrifuged and washed with $40~\mathrm{mL}$ fresh THF two times and vacuum dried for spectroscopic characterization.
Synthesis of Amine Grafted Composite (Amine-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC) (PSM-2): For the second step of PSM with amines, the prepared samples after PSM1 (without drying) were dispersed in $40~\mathsf{m L}$ THF at ${\mathsf{l}}{\mathsf{-}}4{\mathsf{\Omega}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ Then, three equivalents of ED $(0.328~\mathrm{mL})$ , DETA $(0.5~\mathsf{m L})$ , TAEA $(0.72~\mathrm{mL})$ , or PEI $(2.8~\mathsf{m L})$ (with respect to the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathsf{B D C}}^{2-})$ ) were added to the cold solution and allowed to react for $\textsf{l h}$ in the ice bath and then 30 additional min at RT under stirring. After the reaction, the product was washed with $80~\mathrm{mL}$ MeOH and vacuum-dried. The sample was activated at ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under dynamic vacuum before assessing the porosity and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption at different temperatures.
Synthesis of Amine Grafted Composite Washed (Amine-Ac-NH-Cr-BDCWashed): The product obtained from PSM-2, ${\approx}480~\mathrm{mg}$ (without drying) was dispersed in $40\mathsf{m L}$ MeOH and $4\mathsf{m L}$ DIPEA. The samples were shaken vigorously in a vortex machine for 30 min to deprotonate the amines and remove the excess $\mathsf{B r^{-}}$ from the framework. The product was washed with $80~\mathrm{mL}$ MeOH and vacuum-dried. The sample was activated at ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under dynamic vacuum before assessing the porosity and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption at different temperatures.
Synthesis of Amine Impregnated $N H_{2}$ -Cr-BDC: $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{100}}\mathrm{~mg}$ of activated ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -Cr-BDC is added to a mixture of 8 mL cyclohexane and $0.78\mathrm{mL}$ TAEA or $0.2~\mathrm{mL}$ PEI respectively and stirred for $5\mathrm{\min}$ . The composites are centrifuged and washed with excess cyclohexane and vacuum dried at RT. The samples were activated at ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ under dynamic vacuum to access the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption performance at $373\ K.$
Synthesis of Water-Soaked Composites: About $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{100}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{m}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{g}}$ of TAEA-Ac- and PEI-Ac-NH-Cr-BDC-washed are soaked in $70~\mathsf{m L}$ water in a $75m L$ centrifuge tube and shaken in a vortex shaker for $\textsf{l h}$ . The composites are then centrifuged and washed with water one time and MeOH one time and vacuum dried at RT for 1 day. The sample was activated at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ under dynamic vacuum before assessing the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption at $373\mathrm{~K~}$
